# Git Baseline

## Version control

- Use short-lived `feature/<short-desc>` (optional `feature/be-…` / `feature/fe-…`) cut from `main`. Never push directly to `main`/`master`. Worktree/branch lifecycle below.
- Persistent helper slots only: `agent/runtime`, `scratch/debug`, `local/adhoc` — see [`docs/handoffs/worktree-branch-topology.md`](../../docs/handoffs/worktree-branch-topology.md). Never push `local/adhoc`.
- Keep commits small and atomic — one logical change per commit.

### Two lanes: implementation vs fast-track docs (always on)

Choose the lane by **what the diff touches** — full commands in [`docs/handoffs/worktree-branch-topology.md`](../../docs/handoffs/worktree-branch-topology.md):

- **Standard lane — any code change, or mixed code + docs.** One `feature/<short-desc>` branch/worktree per task, cut from latest `main` (`git worktree add .worktrees/<task> -b feature/<desc> origin/main`), then `pre-commit install` (hooks are per-checkout). Atomic conventional commits → sync before merge (`git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main`; Merge Queue is dormant on this user-owned repo) → push → PR → land on green. **Close on merge (required):** `python agent-runtime/scripts/git/worktree_gc.py --close <task>` — prunes, verifies merged (incl. squash) + clean, removes worktree + branch.
- **Fast-track lane — non-code only.** Eligible: `*.md` / `*.mdc` / `*.txt` and other document files, `docs/**`, `.cursor/rules/**`, `.cursor/skills/**` — with **zero** edits to code (`.py`, `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.js`, `.sql`, `.sh`, or code configs). Short branch → commit → push → `gh pr create` → immediate self-merge `gh pr merge --squash --delete-branch --admin`. Still a PR: keeps CI (where paths trigger it), audit trail, and revert-via-PR — it just skips the worktree and review ceremony. **If the diff touches any code file, it is the standard lane.**

Both lanes: **never push to `main` directly**; every change lands via a PR (§ Pull requests).

### Checkout preflight — open the tree before you trust it (always on)

`worktree_gc.py` governs the **close** half of the lifecycle. This governs the **open**
half. Before the first edit in any session, and before starting any task in a checkout you
did not just create, run:

```bash
python agent-runtime/scripts/git/checkout_preflight.py --fetch
```

`FAIL` means the checkout will give you wrong answers — fix it before editing, do not work
around it. The five blocking conditions:

| Check | Fails when | Why it matters |
|-------|-----------|----------------|
| `STALE_BASE` | ≥50 commits or ≥7 days behind `origin/main` | You are reading source that has since changed. Rediscovering a fix that already landed is the most expensive failure this catches. |
| `PRIMARY_TREE` | the primary working directory is not on `main` | It is the default tree every fresh agent reads. Task work belongs in `.worktrees/`. |
| `MAIN_LOCATION` | `main` is checked out in a side worktree | Git then refuses to check out `main` in the primary tree, stranding it on a feature branch indefinitely. |
| `WORKTREE_LOCATION` | a worktree lives outside `.worktrees/` or `.claude/worktrees/` | A worktree at the repo root is read as project source by every `find`, `grep`, and test-collection walk, at whatever commit it was abandoned on. |
| `NESTED_CLONE` | a stray `.git` **directory** exists below the repo root | A separate repository with its own history, invisible to `git status`. |

`WARN` (dirty tree, worktree count over the soft limit, stale `origin` ref) is drift
telemetry — schedule a sweep, keep working.

**This is enforced, not advised.** `.claude/hooks/checkout_preflight_gate.py` runs on every
`Edit`/`Write`/`MultiEdit`/`NotebookEdit` and blocks the write on any blocking `FAIL`.
Override with `JULI_SKIP_CHECKOUT_PREFLIGHT=1` only with a stated reason — it is visible in
the transcript. The gate fails **open** on internal error and never blocks on `WARN`.

Why a gate and not a rule: the correct lifecycle has been written here since #666, and on
2026-08-11 the primary directory was still found 90 commits and 8.5 days behind `main`,
with 46 dirty files, two worktrees abandoned at the repo root, 73 registered worktrees and
61 unmerged branches. Prompt discipline had already been tried.

### Cleanup autonomy & safety (always on)

- The agent may auto-run prune/close for worktrees/branches **it created** that are **merged AND clean**. It must **surface and confirm** before deleting anything human-created, anything with uncommitted or unpushed work, or a branch whose PR is closed-not-merged. Never delete `agent/runtime`, `scratch/debug`, `local/adhoc`, or `main`. Force flags (`-D`, `--force`, `push -f`) always need explicit human sign-off.
- `worktree_gc.py --report` classifies every worktree/branch (safe-to-close / needs-confirm / keep) and deletes nothing; `--sweep` closes all merged+clean at once, listing the rest for confirmation.
- Conventional commits: `<type>: <description>` — types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `ci`.
- **Local pre-commit required** in every checkout/worktree: `pip install pre-commit` then `pre-commit install` (see topology handoff). Runs **ruff check + format** on staged Python under `backend/`, `tests/`, `scripts/` only; **commit-msg** enforces conventional types above. Mypy, Bandit, Gitleaks, and full tests stay in CI / Merge Queue.
- Tag releases and maintain a changelog.

## Pull requests

- All production changes go through PRs — no exceptions (including fast-track docs).
- PR description summarizes **why** and what it affects; include a test plan.
- Keep PRs reviewable (prefer <400 lines).
- **Standard-lane** PRs get review: reviewers check correctness, readability, test coverage, and security. **Fast-track** (non-code) PRs may self-merge without review (`--admin`) — the diff is document-only, so audit + revert-via-PR are the safeguard, not human review. Any code file in the diff disqualifies fast-track.

## Research & reuse

- Prefer existing repo patterns before adding new ones.
- Confirm library behavior via primary docs (Context7 when Focus selects it).
- Prefer battle-tested libraries over hand-rolled utilities.

## CI/CD

- Three-tier CI + wave free-merge ([ADR-052](../../docs/adr/052-wave-free-merge-deferred-artifact-gate.md)): issue→wave PR **fast** checks (lint/type/secrets + cheap unit + path filters + `policy-checks`; no artifact-gate) with **no up-to-date-with-base requirement**; wave push runs before→after domain-matched integration checks; wave→`main` runs the **full** required suite plus the **artifact-gate**, via Merge Queue `merge_group` when available else sync-before-merge.
- Land via **GitHub Merge Queue** after green PR checks — not direct merge to `main`. Parallel ritual (Isolate vs Parallel, ops lock, enqueue): topology handoff + [`.cursor/rules/issue-workflow.mdc`](issue-workflow.mdc).
- Deploy in small, safe batches; maintain rollback capability.
- Automate repeatable builds, tests, deploys, and migrations.

## Test-first (when practical)

- Prefer red/green/refactor for new behavior and bug fixes.
- Do not weaken flaky tests; make them deterministic.

